course: dictionary
lesson: 10941
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. «Waffle» can be a breakfast cake, or it can mean talk that says very little. The words around it show you which meaning to use. Ask yourself: which meaning makes sense in this sentence?
explain: In Shrek, Donkey never stops talking, and he begs for waffles. Both lines use the same word, but the clues point to different meanings. Talking on and on is a clue for the talk meaning. Begging for something to eat is a clue for the food meaning.
explain: Teachers use waffle about writing too. «Don't waffle — get to the point» means "don't write lots of words that say nothing; say what matters." Informal means relaxed, everyday English, the kind you use with friends rather than in formal writing.
fact: Some words have several meanings, and the sentence tells you which one is meant.
fact: A waffle (noun) is a crisp, flat cake cooked in a hot iron that makes a grid of little squares.
fact: In informal UK English, a waffle (noun) is long talk or writing that says very little.
fact: To waffle (verb) means to talk or write too much without saying anything useful.
fact: In informal US English, to waffle means to keep changing your mind and not deciding.
exercise: In Shrek, «Donkey begs for waffles.» What is a waffle here? | A long, boring talk | Keeping changing your mind | *A crisp, flat cake cooked in a hot iron | A soft woollen blanket
exercise: A teacher says, «Don't waffle — get to the point.» What does waffle mean? | Draw a grid of squares | Cook a flat cake | Decide quickly | *Talk or write too much without saying anything useful
exercise: «Sam keeps waffling and never chooses which team to join.» What does waffling mean here? | Talking very quickly | Running fast | Cooking flat cakes | *Changing his mind again and again without deciding
test: In Shrek, «Donkey never stops talking.» Which meaning of waffle does this clue support? | *Talking too much without saying much | Cooking in a hot iron | Changing your mind about a decision | Breakfast food
test: «Would you like waffles with syrup and berries?» Which meaning of waffle is this? | Long talk that says very little | Talking too much | *A crisp, flat cake cooked in a hot iron | Keeping changing your mind
exam: «Stop waffling about your holiday and tell me what you did.» Which meaning fits best? | Cooking waffles for breakfast | Going on a long walk | *Talking or writing too much without saying anything useful | Changing your mind about a holiday
